1. public/
This folder contains everything ordinary website visitors can access.
public stores the customer-facing website.

// npm install --save-dev @types/node @types/express


2. src/
This is the main Node.js backend folder.
src stores the server-side application code.


src/config/
config stores setup and connection settings.
supabase.js will connect Node.js to Supabase.


src/routes/
Routes define the URLs that the server accepts.


src/controllers/
Controllers contain the actual logic executed by routes.
The route receives the request, then calls the controller to decide what the server should do with a request.




src/middleware/
Middleware runs between the request and the final controller.
Middleware sits between Express and the response.
    Check authentication
    Validate data
    errorHandler
we want to reject any booking where:
    Name is empty
    Passengers < 1
    Date is in the past
Why middleware often uses exports directly e.g exports.logger = ()=>{...}
    A middleware file often contains only one middleware.
Then why do we import with { }? 
    What gets exported is actually an object. { logger: logger }
    then const { logger } = require(objectUrl);




src/services/
The service is responsible for fetching the correct business data.
Services contain reusable business logic.
Services perform reusable work that controllers need preventung controllers from becoming too large.
    Suppose later we want the home page to show:
        Featured vehicles
        Three latest testimonials
        Company statistics
    Question: Which layer should retrieve that information?




src/utils/
This contains small helper functions.
Format telephone numbers
Format dates
asyncHandler: receives a controller function and returns a new Express middleware function.


app.use()
I'm delegating this responsibility to another module.



2. exports.errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {};
            or
    const errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {};
    exports.errorHandler = errorHandler;
            or
    module.exports = { getServices };
    
    
The catch(err) block only triggers if something completely unrelated crashes, like a network failure,
 a server blackout, or a syntax typo in your JavaScript code.






3. src/config/env.js
At the very top of app.js, after loading dotenv: e.g require('./config/env');
    Because we don't need anything from it.
    We simply want the file to execute once during startup.
    When Node.js imports a file, it executes it.
    If everything is configured correctly...



// Now an error can specify whether it is a 400, 404, 409, or 500 response.




Promise.all()
    receives an array of promises and waits until all succeed.
        await Promise.all([
            checkServiceExists(bookingData.service_id),
            checkVehicleExists(bookingData.vehicle_id)
        ]);

Why check existence manually if foreign keys already exist?
Without our check, the customer might receive technical message handlers.
    insert or update on table "bookings" violates foreign key constraint vs  "message": "Selected vehicle was not found.",
'


A good validation file usually follows this process:
Receive input
   ↓
Allow only expected             fields Object.fromEntries()
   ↓
Check required fields           !vehicle.name || typeof vehicle.name !
   ↓
Check data types
   ↓
Check formats
   ↓
Check ranges and business rules
   ↓
Clean the values
   ↓
Pass cleaned data forward

